

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Both it is — shared module, two sinks.

  Next fork, and it's the expensive one: [1mwhere browser logs actually end up.[0m The Node side is settled by default
  (structured lines to stdout/stderr, collected by whatever runs the process). The browser has no such default — a log
  that stays in the user's devtools console is invisible to you in production, so this choice is the difference between
  "logging exists" and "we can debug production issues."

  - [1mConsole only, structured[0m — zero infrastructure, works today, and gives you consistent structured output you can ask
    a user to paste. Costs: you cannot see anything proactively. Cheap to change later — adding a transport behind the
    same logger interface is additive.
  - [1mPOST to a collect endpoint we define[0m — real remote visibility. Costs: an endpoint that doesn't exist yet
    (server-side work outside this repo), a batching + retry + failure story, and the payload becomes a contract between
    the two sides. Expensive to change later.
  - [1mThird-party service (Sentry and similar)[0m — remote visibility without building the receiving end, plus grouping and
    stack traces for free. Costs: a dependency and an external account in a repo that currently has zero dependencies,
    and log contents leave your infrastructure — which matters given the credential surface I flagged.

  [1mMy recommendation: console only, structured, with the transport left as a seam.[0m The logger interface is the thing
  worth designing carefully now; the destination is genuinely additive afterward. Picking a transport before we know
  what the production issues look like means building batching and retry for log lines nobody has read yet. If you
  already have a collector or a Sentry account, though, that changes the calculus and I'd wire to it directly.
